# token-auth bootstrap

Bearer-token ForwardAuth for the public AI API routes. See `values.yaml` for
why the previous in-routing-rule token match had to be replaced.

## Create the token Secret (never committed)

`TOKENS_JSON` maps each public hostname to the bearer token that opens it.
Per-host, so ollama's token does not also open hermes:

```
kubectl create namespace homelab-token-auth
kubectl -n homelab-token-auth create secret generic token-auth-secrets \
  --from-literal=TOKENS_JSON='{"infer.smugiwara.com":"<ollama-token>","hermes.smugiwara.com":"<hermes-token>"}'
```

Reuse the tokens from the old `helm/ollama/values.secret.yaml` and
`helm/hermes/values.secret.yaml` if you want existing clients to keep
working unchanged — the header they send is identical. Those two files are
now unused by any chart and can be deleted once this is in place.

Restart to pick up a changed Secret (it is read once at startup):

```
kubectl -n homelab-token-auth rollout restart deploy/token-auth-server
```

## Verify

```
# no token -> 401
curl -so /dev/null -w '%{http_code}\n' https://infer.smugiwara.com/api/tags

# correct token -> 200
curl -so /dev/null -w '%{http_code}\n' \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer <ollama-token>" https://infer.smugiwara.com/api/tags

# ollama's token must NOT open hermes -> 401
curl -so /dev/null -w '%{http_code}\n' \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer <ollama-token>" https://hermes.smugiwara.com/v1/models
```

## Adding another gated endpoint

1. Add the hostname and its token to `TOKENS_JSON`.
2. Add a `forwardauth` Middleware to that stack's namespace (Middlewares are
   namespaced and k3s's Traefik runs with `allowCrossNamespace` off, so each
   stack needs its own object — see `helm/ollama/templates/middleware.yaml`).
3. Annotate the Ingress with `<namespace>-forwardauth@kubernetescrd`.

A hostname with no entry in `TOKENS_JSON` is rejected, so step 1 cannot be
forgotten in a way that leaves the route open.
